  19:     Fill each background chamber with the majority color of the colored marker pixels touching it.
  20: 
  21:     Input structure
  22:     - A 2-D grid has one dominant background color, one wall color, and marker colors.
  23:     - Background pixels form 4-connected chambers separated by wall and marker pixels.
  24:     Rule
  25:     - Infer the background as the unique most frequent color and the wall as the unique most frequent remaining color.
  26:     - For each 4-connected background chamber, collect the distinct orthogonally adjacent marker pixels.
  27:     - Recolor that whole chamber to the unique majority marker color, preserving all non-background pixels.
  28:     Key insight
  29:     - The colored pixels are boundary votes for adjacent empty regions; the grey wall structure only separates the regions.
  30:     """
  31:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)
  32:     assert g.ndim == 2
  33: 
  34:     flat_counts = Counter(map(int, g.ravel()))
  35:     colors = list(flat_counts)
  36:     bg = _unique_most_common(flat_counts, colors, "background")
  37:     wall = _unique_most_common(flat_counts, [color for color in colors if color != bg], "wall")
  38:     marker_colors = set(colors) - {bg, wall}
  39:     assert marker_colors, "expected marker colors besides background and wall"
  40: 
  41:     out = g.copy()
